# Website formatting

How the website writes numbers, money, times and stages. Every page uses these,
so a count reads the same on the atlas, the board and a problem's page.

## Note
A count with thousands separated: 102819 is 102,819.

## To formatCount (n: Int) -> Text:
    If n is less than 0:
        Return "-" + formatCount(0 - n).
    If n is less than 1000:
        Return "{n}".
    Return formatCount(n / 1000) + "," + zfill("{n % 1000}", 3).

## Note
Micro-dollars as money, to the tenth of a cent below a dollar: 1250 is $0.0013,
5000000 is $5.00.

## To formatMoney (micros: Int) -> Text:
    If micros is at least 1000000:
        Return "$" + formatCount(micros / 1000000) + "." + zfill("{(micros % 1000000) / 10000}", 2).
    Return "$0." + zfill("{(micros + 50) / 100}", 4).

## Note
A Unix-milliseconds time as a UTC date and minute, 2026-11-22 20:55, by the
civil-from-days algorithm; zero is never.

## To formatStamp (ms: Int) -> Text:
    If ms is at most 0:
        Return "never".
    Let secs be ms / 1000.
    Let z be secs / 86400 + 719468.
    Let era be z / 146097.
    Let doe be z - era * 146097.
    Let yoe be (doe - doe / 1460 + doe / 36524 - doe / 146096) / 365.
    Let doy be doe - (365 * yoe + yoe / 4 - yoe / 100).
    Let mp be (5 * doy + 2) / 153.
    Let day be doy - (153 * mp + 2) / 5 + 1.
    Let mutable month be mp + 3.
    Let mutable year be yoe + era * 400.
    If month is greater than 12:
        Set month to month - 12.
        Set year to year + 1.
    Let minutes be (secs % 86400) / 60.
    Return "{year}-" + zfill("{month}", 2) + "-" + zfill("{day}", 2) + " " + zfill("{minutes / 60}", 2) + ":" + zfill("{minutes % 60}", 2).

## Note
The factory's stages in the order work moves through them, each with the words
the website uses for it.

## To stageOrder () -> Seq of Text:
    Return ["ranked", "formalizing", "formalized", "in_progress", "partial", "solved", "refuted", "invalid_formalization"].

## To stageLabel (stage: Text) -> Text:
    Let labels be ["Ranked", "Formalizing", "Formalized", "In progress", "Partial", "Solved", "Refuted", "Invalid statement"].
    Let mutable i be 0.
    Repeat for s in stageOrder():
        Set i to i + 1.
        If s is equal to stage:
            Return item i of labels.
    Return stage.

## Note
The first characters of a hash, enough to tell entries apart on screen.

## To shortHash (h: Text) -> Text:
    Return truncate(h, 13).

## Note
A trail verdict as the website words it. A worker's `verified` is a claim until
the trusted verifier attests it, and the timeline says so.

## To verdictWords (verdict: Text) -> Text:
    If verdict is equal to "verified":
        Return "claimed proof · awaiting the verifier".
    If verdict is equal to "admitted_axiom":
        Return "proof resting on an admitted lemma".
    If verdict is equal to "infrastructure":
        Return "the toolchain failed".
    Return verdict.

## Note
The command that starts a worker: the contributor's key passed from their own
shell (so it is in no command line and no history), their budget in dollars, and
their focus when they chose one.

## To workerCommand (budget: Text, focus: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable parts be ["docker run --rm -e OPENROUTER_API_KEY", "-e MACSAD_BUDGET_USD=" + trim(budget)].
    If trim(focus) is not equal to "":
        Push "-e MACSAD_FOCUS=" + trim(focus) to parts.
    Push "-e MACSAD_COORDINATOR=https://macsad.com macsad/worker" to parts.
    Return join(parts, " ").
